// Cloth you can name.
//
// A piece of hanging fabric is a wave you have to write down, and nobody knows what wave they want.
// What everybody knows is what a flag looks like in a stiff breeze, and what one looks like on a
// still day.  So this library holds cloth by the state of the air around it:
//
//     import 'cloth' { Flag, Canvas }
//
//     object Flag(1.8, 1.1, 'wind') { material Canvas  translate [0, 0.7, 0] }
//
// **The cloth is real geometry, not a lit lie.**  Each of these is a `parametric` surface, so the
// wave is actually in the surface: the edge against the sky undulates, the shadow it throws is
// wave-shaped, and a light behind it falls on the folds it really has.  Tilting the normal of a flat
// rectangle gets a similar look head-on and gives itself away at the silhouette, which is where the
// eye looks first.
//
// **Cloth is lit from both sides, and that is worth having rather than working around.**  These
// surfaces enclose nothing, so a lamp or a sun behind one lights the face you are looking at.  That
// is what thin fabric does, and it is most of why a flag with the sun behind it reads as cloth
// rather than as painted metal.
//
// **What is not here: cloth that knows what it is lying on.**  A tablecloth draped over a table
// takes its shape from the table, and nothing here looks at the scene around it.  `Sheet` gives a
// rippled horizontal cloth that will sit on a flat top convincingly; a cloth falling over the *edge*
// of something, or caught on a corner, is not a wave and this library will not fake one.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The states, from a dead calm to a gale.  Each is three numbers: how deep the folds are, how many
// of them there are, and how far they lean.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// How deep the folds are, as a fraction of the cloth's *short* side.  A fraction rather than a
// distance is what lets a hand flag and a stadium banner both look right without being told which
// they are: waves that grow deeper without growing longer read as a scale model of themselves.
function ClothSway(state) -> number {
    switch (state) {
        case 'still'  { return 0.045 }
        case 'breeze' { return 0.105 }
        case 'wind'   { return 0.185 }
        case 'gale'   { return 0.260 }
        default { return 0.105 }
    }
}

// How many full folds run across the cloth's free length.  These stay low on purpose: a flag with
// many small ripples reads as corrugated iron, which is the same mistake a sea makes when its waves
// are too short for its size.
function ClothFolds(state) -> number {
    switch (state) {
        case 'still'  { return 1.15 }
        case 'breeze' { return 1.75 }
        case 'wind'   { return 2.55 }
        case 'gale'   { return 3.30 }
        default { return 1.75 }
    }
}

// How far the line of a fold tilts as it crosses the cloth.  Without this the folds run straight and
// parallel, which is what a corrugated sheet does and not what cloth does; the lean is what turns a
// row of ridges into something that looks like it is being blown.
function ClothLean(state) -> number {
    switch (state) {
        case 'still'  { return 0.35 }
        case 'breeze' { return 0.80 }
        case 'wind'   { return 1.30 }
        case 'gale'   { return 1.85 }
        default { return 0.80 }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The cloths.  Each takes its size in scene units, a state, and a variant that moves the folds along
// so that two flags on the same day are not the same flag twice.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A flag, held along its left edge and free at the other three.  It is built with that held edge at
// the origin, running up the Y axis, and the cloth reaching out along +X -- so a mast at the origin
// is a cylinder and nothing has to be lined up by hand.
//
// **The folds grow from the hoist outward**, which is the whole shape of a flag: the held edge cannot
// move at all, the free edge moves most, and the power of 1.4 between them is what keeps the middle
// from looking like a hinge.
primitive Flag(length, height, state = 'breeze', variant = 1) -> parametric {
    deep = ClothSway(state) * min(length, height)
    folds = ClothFolds(state) * 6.283185
    lean = ClothLean(state)
    phase = variant * 1.7

    return parametric {
        u [0, length]
        v [0, height]

        X { u }
        Y { v }
        Z { deep * pow(u / length, 1.4) * sin(folds * u / length + lean * v / height + phase) }
    }
}

// A banner, held along its top edge and hanging.  Built with that edge at the origin along +X and
// the cloth hanging down -Y, so it can be hung from a beam by translating it and nothing else.
//
// The folds grow downward for the same reason a flag's grow outward, and they run *down* the cloth
// rather than across it: a hanging banner ripples in vertical columns, which is what gravity leaves
// once the wind has had its say.
primitive Banner(width, drop, state = 'breeze', variant = 1) -> parametric {
    deep = ClothSway(state) * min(width, drop)
    folds = ClothFolds(state) * 6.283185
    lean = ClothLean(state)
    phase = variant * 2.3

    return parametric {
        u [0, width]
        v [0, drop]

        X { u }
        Y { 0 - v }
        Z { deep * pow(v / drop, 1.4) * sin(folds * u / width + lean * v / drop + phase) }
    }
}

// How high a `Sheet`'s ripples stand above the surface it is lying on, in scene units.  Worked out
// from the same numbers the sheet is, so that it cannot drift away from the cloth it describes: this
// is what to add to `y` to stand something *on* the cloth rather than in it.
function SheetRise(across, along, state) -> number {
    return ClothSway(state) * min(across, along) * 0.55 * 3.24
}

// A loose horizontal cloth: a sheet, a tablecloth's top, a tarpaulin.  Held nowhere, so the ripples
// run right to the edges, and made of two waves crossing rather than one -- a single wave gives
// parallel ridges, which reads as a ploughed field rather than as fabric.
//
// **Its lowest point is at y = 0, not its middle**, so it is laid on a table by translating it to the
// table's top and nothing more.  A wave written the obvious way swings equally either side of nought,
// and half of a cloth laid that way is *inside* whatever it is lying on -- which shows as the table
// coming through the cloth in patches, and looks exactly as wrong as it is.
primitive Sheet(across, along, state = 'breeze', variant = 1) -> parametric {
    deep = ClothSway(state) * min(across, along) * 0.55
    folds = ClothFolds(state) * 6.283185
    phase = variant * 3.1

    return parametric {
        u [0, across]
        v [0, along]

        X { u }
        Y { deep * (1 + sin(folds * u / across + phase)) +
            deep * 0.62 * (1 + sin(folds * 0.73 * v / along - phase * 1.4)) }
        Z { v }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The cloths themselves.  Each is a whole material, meant to be handed to one of the surfaces above.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Heavy cotton duck: a flag, an awning, a sail.  Almost entirely diffuse, because a woven surface
// scatters in every direction; what little specular there is is broad rather than tight, since cloth
// has no flat facets to give a sharp highlight.
Canvas = material {
    pigment color [0.86, 0.84, 0.78]
    specular 0.08
    shininess 6
}

// Silk: light enough to see through a little, with a sheen that runs along the weave.  The
// transparency is what makes it silk rather than painted paper -- a fold that lets a little light
// past reads as thin, and thinness is the whole of what tells these fabrics apart.
//
// There is no `interior` on it, so nothing is bent on the way through.  That is right for cloth: a
// sheet with no thickness to speak of has nothing to refract in, and giving it a glass-like index
// would bend the view through it as though it were a window pane.
Silk = material {
    pigment color [0.80, 0.72, 0.86]
    specular 0.35
    shininess 60
    transparency 0.22
}

// Plain cotton sheeting: matte, and pale without being white.  This is the one to reach for when the
// cloth is not the subject of the picture.
Cotton = material {
    pigment color [0.90, 0.89, 0.86]
    specular 0.05
    shininess 4
}
